      "math_academy_id": "e-14246",
      "problem": "What is the solution to the equation $6^x - 6^5= 0?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $6^5$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{x}-{6}^{5} & =0 \\\\ {6}^{x}-{6}^{5}+{6}^{5} & =0+{6}^{5} \\\\ {6}^{x}-\\cancel{{6}^{5}}+\\cancel{{6}^{5}} & =0+{6}^{5} \\\\ {6}^{x} & ={6}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sides of the equation share the same base $(6),$ we can equate the exponents. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{6}^{x} & ={6}^{5} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${\\color{blue}x} = {\\color{blue}5}.$",
